ded child  abuse and sexual child abuse which has  really  been up to now completely taboo and  almost impossible to speak about  publicly  uh male or female and especially males  just given the way our culture functions  so  it was a really helpful and important  thing for me personally i had done all  the work  on myself many many years ago but i had  never been able to speak publicly i've  been dealing with outreach groups and  other private organizations and this  really gave me the ability to go much  more public  and i think that's that's a huge part  towards uh beginning to  stem some of these crisis is the ability  to speak so you asked me what i would  say  yeah i'd say in this climate unlike the  climate in which i came up  um it's actually much safer to speak um  i really didn't feel at 12 13  like i could talk to anyone i didn't  really feel safe talking to anyone until  my mid-30s  and i certainly didn't go public with it  until after the me too  uh i think that movement gave us all  room to feel quite  comfortable under one big umbrella but i  think for kids  now um who often go silent it's a much  safer culture in which to come out and  and speak to someone even if it's  someone within your your sphere and not  feel like you can't be heard and that's  it's really i honestly never thought i  would see that day in my lifetime so it  is a very big  change in the way the culture is working  around these issues  well alex i'm so sorry you had to go  through that and i'm i'm in awe that  you've managed to use that experience to  do so much good in terms  of this campaign and speaking out as you  do we mentioned your documentary showbiz  kids and that you stepped away from the  spotlight to make documentaries like  that  knowing everything you know and  everything you've experienced would you  let  your own kids join the entertainment  industry today if they asked you  you know the film that i made i didn't  want it to be a  black and white because the experiences  aren't black and white i was on broadway  at 12 13  co-starring in a big broadway show with  joel brenner and constance tower and it  was  magic it was a dream come true and i was  in heaven i went on to do peter pan with  sandy duncan for years just  lovely experiences that i wouldn't i  wouldn't exchange for anything  but the lows are quite low and they do  impact you and they do  have consequences not only for you but  for your family  um i have three kids uh they're in the  arts to some degree some aren't  but i have not been faced with this  dilemma the way my mother was  my mother wasn't a stage mom so i was  all singing all dancing by five  i mean i was not gonna stop so  she had to face you know that that  decision does does she let me continue  or not  someone like mara wilson cameron boyce  who is very sadly passed on we're just  born to be  doing this evan rachel um todd bridges  and yet um thankfully i have not uh  had to face this dilemma myself with my  own kids i honestly  not to beat around the bush i don't  think i would honestly i think that if  my kids came to me and asked me if they  could enter the business  i really don't um it's probably largely  to do with my own experiences but i  would probably say to them  wait until you're 18. if i had a kid  like cameron or martin like  almost like myself who's just clearly  born to do that  then i would actually roll up my sleeves  and jump in but it would be a big  commitment  fair enough so let me talk about  uh keanu reeves and yourself who made  your name in this in this movie  so many years ago you remained friends  all these years even though your careers  took kind of  different journeys different paths  different directions i have to ask the  question do you ever regret the fact  that you're not john wick  yes i think i would have made a i think  i'm clearly  much better suited to play john wick and  i think that that'll will probably get  sorted out  maybe john wick four he finds his  long-lost partner  uh alex winter there let's just talk  seriously before we run out of time  elections coming up soon you had a tweet  go viral this week about amy coney  barrett calling her out  you're often very critical about trump  on twitter it's where you and i  discovered each other  have you always been this political or  has the current moment just  radicalized you alex no i came up in a  very politically active family i had  a lot of close family members who were  journalists um  i was you know protesting spiro agnew as  a child  uh i was you know handing out mcgovern  flyers door to door  so this is in my blood and uh i find  twitter very useful actually i think  that it's a you know like anything else  with the internet can be used very  poorly  um it can be used for propaganda but it  can also use be used to join people  together  and to get word out and to help with  voting and voting rights and to now  you know the work that we're doing in  swing states try to get the vote out  i voted absentee i voted uh over a week  ago but  there's stuff that i can do every single  day to try to help make sure we correct  the current climate that we're in  and uh get back on track as a country  hopefully  here's a question you're a dual citizen  of the united states in the uk as i am  now  um if the election goes the way you  don't want it to go do you think you'll  be heading for some kind of escape zone  people with dual nationalities often  envied by other americans because  they've got somewhere to escape to  apparently  i mean i miss london because i love it  so much i i only envy their national  health  and it's it's ludicrous that a country  this big does not have uh anything like  that and it's  it's a major problem that has to be  resolved  but i'm not a quitter you know i i love  america and  um i've loved being here and i love the  work that i do here  and i believe very much in this country  and i don't believe in just in just  bailing out on  on something if it's not going my way i  think that's actually the time to roll  up your sleeves and  and protect the things that matter to  you  alex we've got 10 seconds left yay or  nay will there be a bill in ted 4  gosh i don't know i mean maybe if i end  up taking over keanu's role in john wick  then  you know we'll just he'll play bill and  i'll play ted and the world will get  very strange  fair enough we'll see what happens alex  thank you so much uh for taking time out  to speak with us especially on some of  the important topics  we covered take care of yourself media  you too  good to be here
 

